+\section{Cost model, tour rule and the bundle head}
+\label{sec:supp_method}
+
+\paragraph{The four tour-rule caps.} A tour carries at least $230$ parcels: below
+one vehicle load the surrogate's out-of-fold error more than doubles, to $7.1\%$.
+It carries at most $556$ stops, the $99$th percentile of the training pool, and
+covers at most $159$~km$^2$ of service area, above which only $6.4\%$ of training
+rows lie and the bias drifts negative, averaging $-4.3\%$ over the
+$159$--$358$~km$^2$ range. The members' convex hull may not exceed $1.22$ times
+their summed area, the largest ratio observed in the pool.
+
+\paragraph{The bundle head.} The head is certified in a bin only if that bin holds
+at least $6$ labels and its absolute out-of-fold bias is at most $5\%$, which is
+$21$ of $235$ bins, with $2.85\%$ out-of-fold MAPE ($2.71\%$ occurrence-weighted)
+and $1.35\%$ absolute bias. Pool-wide, including the uncertified bins, it reaches
+$5.37\%$ MAPE at $+0.57\%$ bias. Of the pooled tours that occur in the optimized
+grid it prices $53.9\%$, and $27.2\%$ of the pooled euro ($68.9$ and $53.0\%$ at
+full adoption); the conservative fallback keeps the largest groups.
+
+\section{Optimization: restarts, decoupling audit, dropped stage}
+\label{sec:supp_optim}
+
+Across five random restarts the coordinate-descent objective agreed to within a
+relative $10^{-12}$, so the optimum is stable at the reported operating points,
+although global optimality is not guaranteed. The identity audit of the
+decoupled cells, and the tolerance under which five of its $1{,}656$ pairs are
+accepted, are stated in Section~2.4 of the main text. No parcels,
+routes, or vehicles are shared across providers, and system-wide figures are
+obtained solely by aggregating the independently optimized provider solutions. The
+provider-level smoothing stage of the submitted version is dropped from the
+production path: vehicles do not move between depots, so the depot peaks already
+are the fleet, and redistributing weekdays across a provider's depots cannot
+lower them.
+
+\section{Surrogate accuracy by stage}
+\label{sec:supp_surrogate}
+
+The uncalibrated Daganzo approximation underestimated cost by about $26\%$; the
+global $\alpha$ calibration removed that bias and reached $9.7\%$ MAPE; the
+learned residual captured capacity thresholds, stop density and depot distance,
+reducing the error to $2.95\%$ with near-zero bias. The banded structure of the
+panels reflects the integer tour count $\lceil p/Q \rceil$, which quantizes the
+Daganzo backbone into cost bands, together with families of augmented samples
+sharing a postal-code area; it is a property of the training pool rather than a
+fit artifact, and the postal-code-grouped folds keep all samples of an area on
+the same side of every split.
+
+\section{Frequency mechanism, fleet series and the break-even discount}
+\label{sec:supp_fleet}
+
+\paragraph{Why the mix re-densifies.} At $P = 0.25$ the mean delivery frequency
+rises from $\theta = 0.8$ to $0.9$, from $4.93$ to $5.16$ days in the
+routing-optimal plan and $4.68$ to $4.89$ in the operator plan, for two reasons:
+the penalty scales with $\theta$, so each dropped delivery day costs more as
+adoption grows; and the express residual thins out, from $9.6$ to $7.4\%$ of
+routing cost, until in small depots it no longer fills a vehicle and the residual
+tour of a skipped day costs nearly as much as the regular tour it replaces. At
+$\theta = 1$ the residual disappears altogether and the mean frequency drops to
+$3.10$ and $3.27$ days. The step from $\theta = 0.9$ to $1$ is therefore not a
+threshold in the willingness distribution but the price of the last non-willing
+customers.
+
+\paragraph{Fleet.} The Monday--Saturday coefficient of variation of the system
+fleet falls from $0.139$ in the baseline to $0.023$ at $(0.25, 1)$ and $0.044$ at
+$(0.5, 1)$, that is by $84$ and $68\%$, with a grid maximum of $87\%$ at
+$(0, 0.7)$. The system's own peak day falls by $17.5\%$ at $(0, 1)$, against
+$16.9\%$ for the summed depot peak. Stage~2 is a local search and the gap it
+leaves is small but real: at $(0, 1)$ its summed depot peak of $1{,}030$ vehicles
+stands against a flat-profile lower bound of $980$, and at $(0.25, 1)$ the
+residual gap is $32$ peak vehicles.
+
+\paragraph{The rotation mechanism.} Consolidation pays in the operator lens where
+a depot can rotate delivery days across several cells, so their busy days fall on
+different weekdays and the peak stays flat; a depot serving a single cell has no
+rotation available. Eight of DHL's sixteen depots serve exactly one cell and the
+polish returns them to near-daily delivery, while DHL's largest depot, serving
+thirteen cells, keeps rotated two-day patterns.
+
